Existential quantification of a recursively enumerable binary predicate on the natural numbers is recursively enumerable. The argument dovetails the bounded evaluator of a partial-recursive code.

Decoding a natural number as a pair, asserting the predicate, and encoding the unit is partial recursive, so some code c computes it. Sending n and k to the unit when the bounded evaluation of c, for the number of steps in the second half of k, succeeds on the pair of n with the first half of k, is computable. The domain of the search for such a k is therefore recursively enumerable. Success yields, by soundness of the bounded evaluator, a value in the evaluation of c, and decoding that pair gives a witness of the predicate. Conversely, a witness m places the code of the unit in the evaluation of c on the pair of n and m. Completeness of the bounded evaluator supplies a stage s, and the pair code of m and s is a successful search index.
